export default [["q-fcps-ana4-01","After axillary lymph node clearance a woman's medial scapular border lifts away from the chest wall when she pushes against a wall. Which nerve is injured?",["Long thoracic nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"],"The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior, which holds the scapula against the thorax. Its loss causes a winged scapula."],["q-fcps-ana4-02","A child with a supracondylar fracture of the humerus cannot flex the interphalangeal joint of the thumb or the distal joint of the index finger. Which nerve branch is injured?",["Anterior interosseous nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve","Recurrent branch of the median nerve","Deep branch of the ulnar nerve"],"The anterior interosseous nerve supplies flexor pollicis longus and the radial half of flexor digitorum profundus. Injury gives an inability to make the OK sign."],["q-fcps-ana4-03","Which artery gives off the subscapular artery?",["Third part of the axillary artery","First part of the axillary artery","Second part of the axillary artery","Brachial artery"],"The third part of the axillary artery gives the subscapular artery and the anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries."],["q-fcps-ana4-04","Which structures pass through the quadrangular space?",["Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery","Radial nerve and profunda brachii artery","Suprascapular nerve and artery","Circumflex scapular artery only"],"The quadrangular space is bounded by teres minor, teres major, the long head of triceps and the humerus. It transmits the axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral vessels."],["q-fcps-ana4-05","Which nerve runs in the spiral groove of the humerus together with the profunda brachii artery?",["Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"The radial nerve lies in the radial (spiral) groove on the posterior surface of the humerus with the profunda brachii vessels."],["q-fcps-ana4-06","A patient fractures the hook of the hamate. Which structure is most at risk?",["Deep branch of the ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Superficial radial nerve","Palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve"],"The deep branch of the ulnar nerve curves around the hook of the hamate and can be injured or compressed there."],["q-fcps-ana4-07","The proximal pole of the scaphoid is prone to avascular necrosis after a waist fracture because:",["Its blood supply enters distally and runs retrograde","It has no ligamentous attachments","It is covered entirely by articular cartilage and has no periosteum","The radial artery does not supply the carpus"],"Branches of the radial artery enter the distal part of the scaphoid and supply the proximal pole by retrograde flow, so a waist fracture can devascularise it."],["q-fcps-ana4-08","A man has a painless thickening of the palmar skin with progressive flexion of the ring and little fingers. Which structure is involved?",["Palmar aponeurosis","Flexor retinaculum","Extensor expansion","Flexor digitorum superficialis tendon sheath"],"Dupuytren contracture is fibrosis and shortening of the palmar aponeurosis, classically affecting the ring and little fingers."],["q-fcps-ana4-09","Which thenar muscle abducts the thumb at the carpometacarpal joint and is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve?",["Abductor pollicis brevis","Abductor pollicis longus","Adductor pollicis","First dorsal interosseous"],"Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle supplied by the recurrent branch. Abductor pollicis longus is supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve."],["q-fcps-ana4-10","A patient has weakness of knee extension and loss of the knee jerk. Which nerve is most likely affected?",["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"],"The femoral nerve (L2 to L4) supplies quadriceps femoris and forms the afferent and efferent limbs of the knee jerk."],["q-fcps-ana4-11","A man has burning pain and numbness over the anterolateral thigh after wearing a tight belt. Which nerve is compressed?",["Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","Femoral nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Genitofemoral nerve"],"Meralgia paraesthetica is entrapment of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve as it passes under or through the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine."],["q-fcps-ana4-12","A patient develops compartment syndrome of the anterior compartment of the leg. Loss of sensation is most likely in the:",["First web space of the foot","Sole of the foot","Lateral border of the foot","Heel"],"The deep fibular nerve runs in the anterior compartment and supplies the skin of the first web space between the great and second toes."],["q-fcps-ana4-13","Compression of the tibial nerve behind the medial malleolus causes pain and numbness in the sole of the foot. This condition is called:",["Tarsal tunnel syndrome","Morton neuroma","Plantar fasciitis","Meralgia paraesthetica"],"The tibial nerve passes under the flexor retinaculum in the tarsal tunnel. Compression causes burning pain and sensory loss in the sole."],["q-fcps-ana4-14","The posterior cruciate ligament prevents:",["Posterior displacement of the tibia on the femur","Anterior displacement of the tibia on the femur","Hyperextension of the knee only","Valgus stress on the knee"],"The posterior cruciate ligament stops the tibia from sliding backwards on the femur, for example in a dashboard injury."],["q-fcps-ana4-15","Why is the medial meniscus injured more often than the lateral meniscus?",["It is firmly attached to the medial collateral ligament","It is larger than the lateral meniscus","It has a richer blood supply","It is not attached to the tibia"],"The medial meniscus is fixed to the deep part of the medial collateral ligament and is less mobile, so it is more vulnerable to rotational injuries."],["q-fcps-ana4-16","Which muscle is the only abductor of the vocal cords?",["Posterior cricoarytenoid","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Cricothyroid","Thyroarytenoid"],"Posterior cricoarytenoid opens the glottis by rotating the arytenoid cartilages laterally."],["q-fcps-ana4-17","Which muscle tenses the vocal cords by tilting the thyroid cartilage forward, and which nerve supplies it?",["Cricothyroid, external laryngeal nerve","Thyroarytenoid, recurrent laryngeal nerve","Posterior cricoarytenoid, recurrent laryngeal nerve","Cricothyroid, internal laryngeal nerve"],"Cricothyroid is the only intrinsic laryngeal muscle supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve."],["q-fcps-ana4-18","Where is an emergency cricothyroidotomy performed?",["Through the cricothyroid membrane","Between the first and second tracheal rings","Through the thyrohyoid membrane","Below the cricoid cartilage at the fourth tracheal ring"],"The cricothyroid membrane lies between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages and is subcutaneous, so it gives the fastest airway access."],["q-fcps-ana4-19","Recurrent epistaxis from the anterior part of the nasal septum usually arises from which area?",["Kiesselbach plexus","Sphenopalatine artery territory","Posterior ethmoidal artery territory","Inferior turbinate"],"Kiesselbach plexus (Little's area) is an anastomosis of septal branches of the sphenopalatine, greater palatine, superior labial and anterior ethmoidal arteries."],["q-fcps-ana4-20","The maxillary sinus drains into which part of the nose?",["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"],"The maxillary sinus opens into the hiatus semilunaris in the middle meatus, high on its medial wall, so it drains poorly against gravity."],["q-fcps-ana4-21","The nasolacrimal duct opens into the:",["Inferior meatus","Middle meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"],"The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the inferior meatus beneath the inferior concha."],["q-fcps-ana4-22","After a blow to the eye a patient cannot look upwards, has diplopia and numbness of the cheek. Which finding best explains this?",["Entrapment of the inferior rectus in an orbital floor fracture","Fracture of the lamina papyracea with medial rectus palsy","Optic canal fracture","Fracture of the zygomatic arch"],"In a blow-out fracture the inferior rectus and orbital fat herniate into the maxillary sinus. The infraorbital nerve in the floor also causes cheek numbness."],["q-fcps-ana4-23","Which cranial nerve has the longest intracranial course and is a false localising sign in raised intracranial pressure?",["Abducens nerve","Trochlear nerve","Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve"],"The abducens nerve has a long course over the petrous tip and is easily stretched, giving lateral gaze palsy in raised intracranial pressure."],["q-fcps-ana4-24","Which cranial nerve emerges from the dorsal surface of the brainstem?",["Trochlear nerve","Abducens nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Vagus nerve"],"The trochlear nerve is the only cranial nerve to leave the dorsal brainstem and it crosses to supply the contralateral superior oblique."],["q-fcps-ana4-25","The pterygopalatine ganglion receives preganglionic parasympathetic fibres from which nerve?",["Greater petrosal nerve","Chorda tympani","Lesser petrosal nerve","Oculomotor nerve"],"The greater petrosal branch of the facial nerve synapses in the pterygopalatine ganglion and supplies lacrimal, nasal and palatine glands."],["q-fcps-ana4-26","The otic ganglion receives its preganglionic fibres from the:",["Lesser petrosal nerve (glossopharyngeal)","Greater petrosal nerve","Chorda tympani","Vagus nerve"],"The tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve forms the lesser petrosal nerve, which synapses in the otic ganglion and supplies the parotid gland."],["q-fcps-ana4-27","A lesion of the facial nerve in the internal acoustic meatus causes all of the following EXCEPT:",["Loss of general sensation over the face","Loss of taste from the anterior two thirds of the tongue","Hyperacusis","Dry eye"],"Facial sensation is carried by the trigeminal nerve. A proximal facial lesion causes facial weakness, dry eye, hyperacusis and loss of anterior tongue taste."],["q-fcps-ana4-28","A stroke causes weakness of the lower face on the left but the forehead is spared. The lesion is:",["Upper motor neuron lesion in the right cerebral hemisphere","Lower motor neuron lesion of the left facial nerve","Lesion of the left facial nucleus","Right trigeminal lesion"],"The upper face receives bilateral cortical input, so an upper motor neuron lesion spares the forehead. A lower motor neuron lesion weakens the whole half of the face."],["q-fcps-ana4-29","The corneal reflex has which afferent and efferent limbs?",["Ophthalmic division of V; facial nerve","Maxillary division of V; facial nerve","Optic nerve; oculomotor nerve","Ophthalmic division of V; oculomotor nerve"],"The nasociliary branch of V1 carries the afferent signal and the facial nerve closes the eye through orbicularis oculi."],["q-fcps-ana4-30","The afferent limb of the gag reflex is carried by the:",["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"The glossopharyngeal nerve carries sensation from the pharyngeal wall and the vagus nerve is the efferent limb."],["q-fcps-ana4-31","A patient has loss of pain and temperature on the right face and the left side of the body, with hoarseness, dysphagia and ataxia. Which artery is occluded?",["Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","Anterior spinal artery","Basilar artery"],"Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome from PICA or vertebral artery occlusion affects the spinal trigeminal nucleus, spinothalamic tract, nucleus ambiguus and cerebellar pathways."],["q-fcps-ana4-32","A patient has a right oculomotor palsy with left hemiparesis. The lesion is in the:",["Right midbrain (Weber syndrome)","Right pons","Left medulla","Right internal capsule"],"In Weber syndrome the lesion involves the oculomotor fascicles and the cerebral peduncle, so there is an ipsilateral III palsy with contralateral hemiparesis."],["q-fcps-ana4-33","Damage to the subthalamic nucleus classically produces:",["Contralateral hemiballismus","Ipsilateral hemiballismus","Resting tremor","Chorea of the face only"],"A lesion of the subthalamic nucleus, often a small vascular lesion, causes violent flinging movements on the opposite side."],["q-fcps-ana4-34","Atrophy of the caudate nucleus is characteristic of:",["Huntington disease","Parkinson disease","Wilson disease","Alzheimer disease"],"Huntington disease causes loss of caudate and putamen neurons, enlarging the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles."],["q-fcps-ana4-35","A patient has a right homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing. The most likely cause is infarction in the territory of the:",["Left posterior cerebral artery","Right posterior cerebral artery","Left anterior cerebral artery","Left middle cerebral artery stem"],"The occipital pole has a dual supply from the middle and posterior cerebral arteries, so macular fibres are often spared in PCA occlusion."],["q-fcps-ana4-36","A lesion of Meyer's loop in the temporal lobe produces:",["Contralateral superior quadrantanopia","Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Ipsilateral blindness"],"Fibres of the lower retina travelling through the temporal lobe form Meyer's loop. Damage gives a 'pie in the sky' defect on the opposite side."],["q-fcps-ana4-37","Where does the corticospinal tract mostly decussate?",["Pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla","Upper pons","Midbrain","At the level of entry in the spinal cord"],"About 85 to 90 percent of corticospinal fibres cross in the pyramidal decussation of the medulla."],["q-fcps-ana4-38","Fibres of the dorsal columns synapse first in which structure?",["Nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus","Ventral posterolateral nucleus","Substantia gelatinosa","Clarke column"],"Dorsal column fibres ascend ipsilaterally and synapse in the gracile and cuneate nuclei of the medulla before crossing as the medial lemniscus."],["q-fcps-ana4-39","Pain and temperature fibres from the right leg cross the midline:",["Within one or two segments of entry, in the anterior white commissure","In the medulla","In the thalamus","In the internal capsule"],"Second-order spinothalamic neurons cross in the anterior white commissure close to their level of entry and ascend on the opposite side."],["q-fcps-ana4-40","The thoracic part of the azygos vein drains into the:",["Superior vena cava at the level of T4","Right brachiocephalic vein","Inferior vena cava","Coronary sinus"],"The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung and enters the SVC at about T4, linking the caval systems."],["q-fcps-ana4-41","Which pericardial sinus lies behind the left atrium?",["Oblique sinus","Transverse sinus","Costomediastinal recess","Coronary sulcus"],"The oblique sinus is a blind recess behind the left atrium bounded by the pericardial reflections around the pulmonary veins and inferior vena cava."],["q-fcps-ana4-42","A surgeon clamps the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk to control bleeding by passing a finger through which space?",["Transverse pericardial sinus","Oblique pericardial sinus","Anterior mediastinum","Costodiaphragmatic recess"],"The transverse sinus lies behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the SVC and left atrium, allowing both vessels to be clamped together."],["q-fcps-ana4-43","In a right-dominant heart the posterior interventricular artery arises from the:",["Right coronary artery","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery"],"In about 70 to 85 percent of people the right coronary artery gives the posterior interventricular artery and supplies the AV node."],["q-fcps-ana4-44","The most common site for pericardiocentesis in an emergency is:",["Left of the xiphoid process, aiming towards the left shoulder","Right second intercostal space","Left fourth intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Suprasternal notch"],"The subxiphoid route avoids the pleura and the internal thoracic vessels and reaches the pericardial sac directly."],["q-fcps-ana4-45","Which structure forms the inferior boundary of Hesselbach's triangle?",["Inguinal ligament","Lateral border of rectus abdominis","Inferior epigastric vessels","Falx inguinalis (conjoint tendon)"],"Hesselbach's triangle is bounded by the inferior epigastric vessels laterally, the rectus abdominis medially and the inguinal ligament inferiorly. Hernias through it are direct."],["q-fcps-ana4-46","A thin patient has postprandial vomiting that improves when lying on the left side or prone. Which part of the duodenum is compressed?",["Third part, between the superior mesenteric artery and aorta","First part, by the gallbladder","Second part, by the pancreatic head","Fourth part, by the inferior mesenteric artery"],"In superior mesenteric artery syndrome the third part of the duodenum is compressed between the SMA and the aorta, usually after rapid weight loss."],["q-fcps-ana4-47","The left renal vein passes between which two structures?",["Superior mesenteric artery and aorta","Aorta and inferior vena cava","Coeliac trunk and aorta","Left ureter and psoas"],"The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta below the origin of the SMA, and can be compressed in the nutcracker phenomenon."],["q-fcps-ana4-48","Which structure marks the boundary between the superior and inferior parts of the rectus sheath's posterior wall?",["Arcuate line","Linea semilunaris","Linea alba","Tendinous intersection"],"Below the arcuate line all aponeuroses pass in front of rectus abdominis, leaving only transversalis fascia behind."],["q-fcps-ana4-49","Lymph from the testis drains to which nodes?",["Para-aortic nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Deep inguinal nodes","Internal iliac nodes"],"The testis develops near the kidney and its lymph follows the testicular vessels to the para-aortic nodes. Scrotal skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes."],["q-fcps-ana4-50","McBurney's point lies:",["One third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus","Midway between the umbilicus and the pubic symphysis","At the midinguinal point","At the tip of the ninth costal cartilage"],"McBurney's point approximates the base of the appendix, a third of the distance along a line from the right ASIS to the umbilicus."],["q-fcps-ana4-51","Which organs are retroperitoneal?",["Second part of the duodenum and the head of the pancreas","Transverse colon and jejunum","Stomach and spleen","Ileum and sigmoid colon"],"The second and third parts of the duodenum, pancreatic head, ascending and descending colon, kidneys and adrenals are retroperitoneal."],["q-fcps-ana4-52","Which structure lies in the free edge of the lesser omentum?",["Portal triad (hepatic artery, portal vein and bile duct)","Left gastric artery only","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery and short gastric vessels"],"The hepatoduodenal ligament forms the free edge of the lesser omentum and contains the portal triad, and the epiploic foramen lies behind it."],["q-fcps-ana4-53","Pain from the appendix is first felt around the umbilicus because it is:",["A midgut structure with visceral afferents to T10","Supplied by somatic nerves from the iliac fossa","Innervated by the ilioinguinal nerve","Supplied by the genitofemoral nerve"],"Early appendicitis causes referred visceral pain at the T10 dermatome. Later, peritoneal irritation causes localised right iliac fossa pain."],["q-fcps-ana4-54","The paraumbilical veins link the portal system to which systemic veins in portal hypertension?",["Epigastric veins","Azygos veins","Inferior rectal veins","Lumbar veins"],"Dilated paraumbilical veins radiating from the umbilicus give caput medusae by connecting the left portal vein to the epigastric veins."],["q-fcps-ana4-55","In a male with a hydrocele the fluid lies in the:",["Tunica vaginalis","Spermatic cord within the pampiniform plexus","Scrotal dartos layer","Epididymal head only"],"A hydrocele is a collection of fluid in the processus vaginalis or tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis."],["q-fcps-ana4-56","Which layer of the spermatic cord is derived from the internal oblique muscle?",["Cremasteric fascia","External spermatic fascia","Internal spermatic fascia","Tunica vaginalis"],"The cremaster and its fascia come from the internal oblique, the external spermatic fascia from the external oblique aponeurosis and the internal spermatic fascia from transversalis fascia."],["q-fcps-ana4-57","DiGeorge syndrome results from failure of development of which pharyngeal pouches?",["Third and fourth","First and second","Second and third only","Fifth and sixth"],"The third pouch forms the thymus and inferior parathyroids and the fourth forms the superior parathyroids, so defects cause T-cell deficiency and hypocalcaemia."],["q-fcps-ana4-58","The stapes, styloid process and facial nerve are derivatives of which pharyngeal arch?",["Second arch","First arch","Third arch","Fourth arch"],"Reichert's cartilage of the second arch gives the stapes, styloid process, lesser horn of the hyoid and the facial nerve supplies its muscles."],["q-fcps-ana4-59","A midline neck swelling that moves upwards on protrusion of the tongue is most likely a:",["Thyroglossal duct cyst","Branchial cyst","Cystic hygroma","Lymph node"],"Thyroglossal duct cysts arise from remnants of the thyroid's descent from the foramen caecum and move with swallowing and tongue protrusion."],["q-fcps-ana4-60","Meckel's diverticulum is a remnant of the:",["Vitelline duct","Urachus","Allantois","Ductus venosus"],"A persistent proximal vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct forms Meckel's diverticulum in the distal ileum."],["q-fcps-ana4-61","Failure of migration of neural crest cells into the distal colon causes:",["Hirschsprung disease","Intussusception","Malrotation","Gastroschisis"],"Hirschsprung disease is aganglionosis of the distal bowel from arrested neural crest migration, causing functional obstruction."],["q-fcps-ana4-62","Horseshoe kidney is usually prevented from ascending to its normal position by the:",["Inferior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Coeliac trunk","Renal artery"],"The isthmus of a horseshoe kidney is trapped beneath the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery during ascent."],["q-fcps-ana4-63","The gubernaculum in the male becomes the:",["Scrotal ligament","Round ligament of the uterus","Ovarian ligament","Cremaster only"],"The gubernaculum guides descent of the testis and persists as the scrotal ligament. In females it forms the round ligament and ovarian ligament."],["q-fcps-ana4-64","Which cell type of the alveolus secretes surfactant?",["Type II pneumocyte","Type I pneumocyte","Clara cell","Alveolar macrophage"],"Type II pneumocytes contain lamellar bodies that store and secrete surfactant and also regenerate type I cells."],["q-fcps-ana4-65","Paneth cells are found in the:",["Crypts of the small intestine","Gastric fundic glands","Colonic surface epithelium","Oesophageal submucosa"],"Paneth cells at the base of the crypts of Lieberkuhn secrete lysozyme and defensins."],["q-fcps-ana4-66","Brunner's glands are characteristic of the:",["Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Colon"],"Mucous Brunner's glands in the duodenal submucosa secrete alkaline mucus to neutralise gastric acid."],["q-fcps-ana4-67","Which epithelium lines most of the urinary tract from the renal calyces to the bladder?",["Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous keratinised","Simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar"],"Transitional epithelium (urothelium) stretches to accommodate changing volume."],["q-fcps-ana4-68","Which liver cells store vitamin A?",["Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells","Kupffer cells","Hepatocytes","Cholangiocytes"],"Stellate cells in the perisinusoidal space store vitamin A and produce collagen in cirrhosis."],["q-fcps-ana4-69","Elastic cartilage is found in the:",["Epiglottis and external ear","Intervertebral disc","Articular surfaces of synovial joints","Costal cartilages"],"Elastic cartilage contains elastic fibres and is found in the epiglottis, pinna and auditory tube."],["q-fcps-ana4-70","Parafollicular C cells of the thyroid secrete:",["Calcitonin","Thyroxine","Parathyroid hormone","Thyroglobulin"],"C cells derive from the neural crest via the ultimobranchial body and secrete calcitonin."],["q-fcps-ana5-01","Which cord of the brachial plexus gives rise to the musculocutaneous nerve?",["Lateral cord","Medial cord","Posterior cord","Upper trunk"],"The lateral cord gives the musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve. The medial cord gives the ulnar nerve and the medial root of the median nerve."],["q-fcps-ana5-02","The posterior cord of the brachial plexus gives rise to which pair of nerves?",["Axillary and radial nerves","Median and ulnar nerves","Musculocutaneous and median nerves","Long thoracic and dorsal scapular nerves"],"The posterior cord receives fibres from all three posterior divisions and ends as the axillary and radial nerves."],["q-fcps-ana5-03","A toddler is lifted by the hand and then holds the forearm pronated and will not use the arm. What is the likely injury?",["Subluxation of the radial head under the annular ligament","Fracture of the clavicle","Dislocation of the shoulder","Fracture of the scaphoid"],"Sudden traction in a young child pulls the radial head out from under the annular ligament (nursemaid's elbow)."],["q-fcps-ana5-04","A dinner-fork deformity of the wrist after a fall suggests:",["Colles fracture with dorsal displacement of the distal radius","Smith fracture with volar displacement","Scaphoid fracture","Monteggia fracture"],"A Colles fracture is a transverse fracture of the distal radius within 2.5 cm of the wrist, with dorsal angulation of the fragment."],["q-fcps-ana5-05","Which structure passes through Guyon's canal?",["Ulnar nerve and artery","Median nerve","Radial artery","Flexor pollicis longus tendon"],"Guyon's canal lies between the pisiform and the hook of the hamate and carries the ulnar nerve and artery into the hand."],["q-fcps-ana5-06","Which of these structures does NOT pass through the carpal tunnel?",["Flexor carpi radialis tendon","Median nerve","Flexor pollicis longus tendon","Tendons of flexor digitorum profundus"],"Flexor carpi radialis runs in its own compartment of the flexor retinaculum, and the tunnel holds nine tendons and the median nerve."],["q-fcps-ana5-07","Which axillary lymph nodes lie medial to pectoralis minor?",["Level III (apical) nodes","Level I nodes","Level II nodes","Pectoral nodes"],"Level I is lateral, level II lies behind, and level III is medial to pectoralis minor. Levels guide surgical clearance."],["q-fcps-ana5-08","The lateral quadrants of the breast drain mainly to the:",["Axillary lymph nodes","Parasternal nodes","Supraclavicular nodes only","Inguinal nodes"],"About 75 percent of lymph drains to the axillary nodes, especially the anterior group. The medial quadrants drain to the parasternal nodes."],["q-fcps-ana5-09","The suspensory ligaments of the breast are called:",["Cooper's ligaments","Scarpa's fascia","Camper's fascia","Colles fascia"],"Fibrous septa from the dermis to the pectoral fascia support the breast, and tethering by cancer causes skin dimpling."],["q-fcps-ana5-10","The sternal angle lies at the level of which structures?",["Second costal cartilage and the T4-T5 intervertebral disc","Third costal cartilage and T6","First costal cartilage and T2","Xiphisternal joint and T9"],"It is a landmark for counting ribs, and at this level the trachea bifurcates, the aortic arch begins and ends, and the azygos arches."],["q-fcps-ana5-11","Which lung has the eparterial bronchus?",["Right lung","Left lung","Both lungs","Neither lung"],"The right upper lobe bronchus arises above the level of the pulmonary artery. Hence the right has three lobes and the left two."],["q-fcps-ana5-12","How many bronchopulmonary segments does the right lung have?",["10","8","9","12"],"Each lung is conventionally described with 10 segments, though the left lung is often considered to have 8 to 10 through fusion."],["q-fcps-ana5-13","In the hilum of the right lung, the arrangement from superior to inferior is:",["Eparterial bronchus, pulmonary artery, hyponarterial bronchus, pulmonary veins","Pulmonary artery, bronchus, veins","Veins, artery, bronchus","Bronchus, veins, artery"],"On the right the eparterial bronchus lies above the pulmonary artery, the lower bronchus is below it and the veins are lowest and in front."],["q-fcps-ana5-14","Aspirated foreign bodies in an upright patient most commonly enter which segment?",["Posterior basal segment of the right lower lobe","Apical segment of the right upper lobe","Medial segment of the right middle lobe","Lingula"],"The right bronchus is wide and straight, and in the upright position the posterior basal segment of the lower lobe is the most dependent. In the supine position the superior segment of the lower lobe or the posterior segment of the upper lobe is more likely."],["q-fcps-ana5-15","The thymus lies in the:",["Anterior (superior) mediastinum","Posterior mediastinum","Middle mediastinum","Inferior mediastinum behind the heart"],"In children the thymus is large and lies in front of the great vessels, and it involutes after puberty."],["q-fcps-ana5-16","The mitral valve is best auscultated at the:",["Apex, fifth left intercostal space in the midclavicular line","Left second intercostal space","Right second intercostal space","Left sternal edge at the fourth intercostal space"],"The apex area corresponds to the mitral valve. The tricuspid is at the lower left sternal edge, the pulmonary at the left second space and the aortic at the right second space."],["q-fcps-ana5-17","Which structure carries part of the right bundle branch across the right ventricle to the anterior papillary muscle?",["Moderator band (septomarginal trabecula)","Chordae tendineae","Crista terminalis","Trabeculae carneae"],"The moderator band conveys the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle."],["q-fcps-ana5-18","The sinoatrial node is located at:",["The upper end of the crista terminalis near the opening of the SVC","The interatrial septum near the coronary sinus","The apex of the ventricle","The base of the aortic valve"],"The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium at the junction of the SVC and the crista terminalis."],["q-fcps-ana5-19","After birth, the foramen ovale closes functionally because of:",["A rise in left atrial pressure above right atrial pressure","Contraction of the septum primum","Closure of the ductus venosus","Increase in right atrial pressure"],"Lung expansion lowers pulmonary resistance and raises left atrial pressure, which presses the septum primum against the septum secundum."],["q-fcps-ana5-20","The ductus venosus becomes the:",["Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum teres","Median umbilical ligament","Medial umbilical ligament"],"The ductus venosus shunts blood from the umbilical vein past the liver, and it closes to form the ligamentum venosum."],["q-fcps-ana5-21","The ligamentum teres of the liver is the remnant of the:",["Left umbilical vein","Right umbilical vein","Ductus venosus","Umbilical artery"],"It lies in the free edge of the falciform ligament and runs from the umbilicus to the left branch of the portal vein."],["q-fcps-ana5-22","The umbilical cord normally contains:",["Two arteries and one vein","One artery and two veins","Two arteries and two veins","One artery and one vein"],"A single umbilical artery is associated with renal and cardiac anomalies."],["q-fcps-ana5-23","The boundaries of the lesser sac include which structure as its anterior wall?",["Lesser omentum and stomach","Pancreas","Left kidney","Transverse mesocolon"],"The lesser sac lies behind the stomach and lesser omentum and communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen."],["q-fcps-ana5-24","The epiploic foramen (of Winslow) has which structure as its anterior boundary?",["Free edge of the lesser omentum containing the portal triad","Inferior vena cava","Caudate lobe of the liver","First part of the duodenum"],"Behind it lies the IVC, above it the caudate process of the liver and below it the first part of the duodenum."],["q-fcps-ana5-25","Which structure is related to the posterior surface of the stomach (the stomach bed)?",["Pancreas and splenic artery","Right kidney","Gallbladder","Aortic arch"],"The stomach bed includes the diaphragm, spleen, left kidney and adrenal, pancreas and transverse mesocolon."],["q-fcps-ana5-26","The right gastric artery is a branch of the:",["Hepatic artery proper","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Superior mesenteric artery"],"The right gastric artery arises from the hepatic artery proper and anastomoses with the left gastric along the lesser curvature."],["q-fcps-ana5-27","Which artery lies posterior to the first part of the duodenum and is a common source of bleeding from a posterior duodenal ulcer?",["Gastroduodenal artery","Splenic artery","Right gastro-omental artery","Cystic artery"],"A posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, causing severe haemorrhage."],["q-fcps-ana5-28","The ampulla of Vater opens into which part of the duodenum?",["Second part, at the major duodenal papilla","First part","Third part","Fourth part"],"The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct open together on the major papilla, surrounded by the sphincter of Oddi."],["q-fcps-ana5-29","According to Couinaud's classification, how many functional segments does the liver have?",["8","4","6","10"],"Each segment has its own portal triad and hepatic vein drainage, and this allows segmental resection."],["q-fcps-ana5-30","The caudate lobe of the liver corresponds to Couinaud segment:",["I","IV","VIII","II"],"Segment I drains directly into the IVC through small veins, so it is spared in Budd-Chiari syndrome."],["q-fcps-ana5-31","The hepatic veins drain into the:",["Inferior vena cava","Portal vein","Right atrium directly","Superior mesenteric vein"],"Three major hepatic veins drain into the IVC just below the diaphragm."],["q-fcps-ana5-32","Which structure lies directly behind the neck of the pancreas and forms the portal vein?",["Superior mesenteric vein joining the splenic vein","Inferior mesenteric artery","Left renal vein","Right renal artery"],"The portal vein is formed behind the pancreatic neck by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins."],["q-fcps-ana5-33","The spleen lies against which ribs?",["9th to 11th","6th to 8th","4th to 6th","10th to 12th"],"The long axis of the spleen follows the 10th rib, and a fracture of the lower left ribs may rupture it."],["q-fcps-ana5-34","The splenorenal ligament contains:",["Splenic vessels and the tail of the pancreas","The left gastric artery","Short gastric vessels","The hepatic artery"],"The tail of the pancreas lies in the splenorenal ligament, so it can be injured during splenectomy."],["q-fcps-ana5-35","Which structure lies directly anterior to the right kidney?",["Second part of the duodenum","Spleen","Jejunum","Stomach"],"The right kidney is related anteriorly to the right suprarenal gland, liver, duodenum and hepatic flexure, while the left is related to the spleen, stomach, pancreas and jejunum."],["q-fcps-ana5-36","Renal stones commonly lodge at which three sites?",["Pelviureteric junction, pelvic brim and vesicoureteric junction","Renal cortex, medulla and papilla","Bladder neck, urethra and meatus","Hilum, calyx and hilum"],"The three constrictions of the ureter are the PUJ, the crossing of the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim and the intramural part at the bladder."],["q-fcps-ana5-37","The ureter is crossed anteriorly in the pelvis by the:",["Uterine artery (water under the bridge) in the female","Internal iliac vein","Obturator nerve","Superior vesical artery in the female"],"The uterine artery crosses above and in front of the ureter near the cervix, so the ureter is at risk in hysterectomy."],["q-fcps-ana5-38","Which fascial structure is the main support of the uterus at the level of the cervix?",["Transverse cervical (cardinal) ligament","Round ligament","Broad ligament","Ovarian ligament"],"The cardinal ligaments are condensations of pelvic fascia and the chief support against prolapse."],["q-fcps-ana5-39","The round ligament of the uterus passes through the:",["Deep inguinal ring to the labium majus","Obturator canal","Greater sciatic foramen","Femoral canal"],"It is the remnant of the gubernaculum and ends in the fat of the labium majus."],["q-fcps-ana5-40","Benign prostatic hyperplasia mainly affects which zone of the prostate?",["Transition zone","Peripheral zone","Central zone","Anterior fibromuscular stroma"],"The transition zone surrounds the urethra, and it causes obstruction. Cancer usually arises in the peripheral zone."],["q-fcps-ana5-41","Rupture of the bulbar urethra leads to extravasation of urine into the:",["Superficial perineal pouch","Deep perineal pouch","Ischiorectal fossa","Retropubic space"],"Urine spreads into the scrotum, penis and lower abdominal wall deep to Scarpa's fascia but is limited by Colles fascia."],["q-fcps-ana5-42","The pudendal canal (Alcock's canal) lies in the wall of which structure?",["Ischioanal (ischiorectal) fossa","Obturator foramen","Femoral triangle","Inguinal canal"],"The canal is formed by obturator internus fascia and contains the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels."],["q-fcps-ana5-43","Which muscle forms the main part of the pelvic diaphragm?",["Levator ani","Obturator internus","Piriformis","Gluteus maximus"],"Levator ani, with coccygeus, supports the pelvic viscera, and its puborectalis part maintains faecal continence."],["q-fcps-ana5-44","Which nerve supplies the external anal sphincter?",["Pudendal nerve (inferior rectal branch)","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Hypogastric nerves","Genitofemoral nerve"],"The external sphincter is skeletal muscle supplied by the inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve."],["q-fcps-ana5-45","Which sacral levels give rise to the pelvic splanchnic nerves?",["S2-S4","L1-L3","S1-S2","S4-S5"],"They carry parasympathetic fibres to the hindgut distal to the splenic flexure, bladder, and erectile tissue."],["q-fcps-ana5-46","A hernia that appears below and lateral to the pubic tubercle is most likely a:",["Femoral hernia","Direct inguinal hernia","Indirect inguinal hernia","Obturator hernia"],"Femoral hernias are more common in women, pass through the femoral canal and have a high risk of strangulation."],["q-fcps-ana5-47","The inguinal ligament is formed by the:",["Free lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis","Internal oblique aponeurosis","Transversalis fascia","Conjoint tendon"],"It runs from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle."],["q-fcps-ana5-48","The anterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by:",["External oblique aponeurosis","Transversalis fascia","Conjoint tendon","Inguinal ligament"],"The posterior wall is transversalis fascia reinforced medially by the conjoint tendon, the roof is the arching fibres of internal oblique and transversus, and the floor is the inguinal ligament."],["q-fcps-ana5-49","The deep inguinal ring is an opening in the:",["Transversalis fascia","External oblique aponeurosis","Internal oblique muscle","Rectus sheath"],"It lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, a landmark that distinguishes indirect from direct hernias."],["q-fcps-ana5-50","Which nerve is at risk during inguinal hernia repair and provides sensation to the root of the penis or mons pubis?",["Ilioinguinal nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Pudendal nerve"],"The ilioinguinal nerve passes through the inguinal canal on the spermatic cord."],["q-fcps-ana5-51","Which ligament of the hip joint is the strongest and prevents hyperextension?",["Iliofemoral ligament","Pubofemoral ligament","Ischiofemoral ligament","Ligamentum teres"],"The Y-shaped ligament of Bigelow lies anteriorly."],["q-fcps-ana5-52","An intramuscular injection into which gluteal quadrant avoids the sciatic nerve?",["Upper outer quadrant","Lower outer quadrant","Upper inner quadrant","Lower inner quadrant"],"The sciatic nerve lies in the lower medial part and the superior gluteal vessels in the upper medial part."],["q-fcps-ana5-53","Which tendon is attached to the calcaneus and is the most frequently ruptured tendon in middle-aged athletes?",["Achilles tendon","Tibialis posterior","Peroneus longus","Quadriceps tendon"],"Rupture is detected by a positive Thompson (squeeze) test, in which the calf squeeze fails to plantar-flex the foot."],["q-fcps-ana5-54","The most commonly sprained ligament of the ankle is the:",["Anterior talofibular ligament","Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Posterior talofibular ligament"],"Inversion injuries damage the weaker lateral ligaments, starting with the anterior talofibular ligament."],["q-fcps-ana5-55","The popliteal artery is related to which bone and is vulnerable in a posterior knee dislocation?",["Posterior surface of the femur and the tibia","Patella","Fibular head only","Talus"],"The deepest structure of the fossa lies against the capsule of the knee joint, so dislocation or supracondylar fracture can damage it."],["q-fcps-ana5-56","The tibial nerve and common fibular nerve arise in the popliteal fossa from the:",["Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"],"The sciatic nerve divides at the upper angle of the fossa."],["q-fcps-ana5-57","Which muscle is the principal plantar flexor of the ankle?",["Gastrocnemius with soleus","Tibialis anterior","Peroneus tertius","Extensor digitorum longus"],"The triceps surae acts via the Achilles tendon and is supplied by the tibial nerve (S1-S2)."],["q-fcps-ana5-58","A positive Trendelenburg sign on the left means weakness of the:",["Left gluteus medius and minimus","Right gluteus medius","Left gluteus maximus","Left iliopsoas"],"The pelvis drops on the opposite side when standing on the affected leg."],["q-fcps-ana5-59","Which vein drains the dorsal venous arch of the foot medially?",["Great saphenous vein","Small saphenous vein","Popliteal vein","Anterior tibial vein"],"The great saphenous vein starts anterior to the medial malleolus and ends in the femoral vein at the saphenofemoral junction."],["q-fcps-ana5-60","The small saphenous vein ends by draining into the:",["Popliteal vein","Femoral vein","Great saphenous vein","Posterior tibial vein"],"It ascends behind the lateral malleolus and pierces the popliteal fascia to join the popliteal vein."],["q-fcps-ana5-61","Which thalamic nucleus relays visual information to the occipital cortex?",["Lateral geniculate nucleus","Medial geniculate nucleus","Ventral posterolateral nucleus","Ventral lateral nucleus"],"The lateral geniculate body receives the optic tract, and the medial geniculate body relays hearing."],["q-fcps-ana5-62","The ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus of the thalamus carries sensation from the:",["Face","Upper limb","Lower limb","Trunk"],"VPL carries body sensation by the medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract, and VPM carries trigeminal sensation."],["q-fcps-ana5-63","The genu of the internal capsule contains:",["Corticobulbar fibres","Corticospinal fibres for the leg","Auditory radiations","Optic radiations"],"The posterior limb carries corticospinal fibres and sensory fibres, and the retrolenticular part carries the optic radiations."],["q-fcps-ana5-64","Which cerebellar peduncle carries the main afferent input from the pons?",["Middle cerebellar peduncle","Superior cerebellar peduncle","Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Medial lemniscus"],"The middle peduncle is composed of pontocerebellar fibres. The superior peduncle is the main efferent pathway."],["q-fcps-ana5-65","The cerebrospinal fluid leaves the fourth ventricle through the:",["Foramina of Luschka and Magendie","Foramen of Monro","Cerebral aqueduct","Central canal only"],"The lateral apertures (Luschka) and the median aperture (Magendie) lead to the subarachnoid space."],["q-fcps-ana5-66","Obstruction of the cerebral aqueduct causes dilatation of which ventricles?",["Lateral and third ventricles","Fourth ventricle only","All four ventricles","Third ventricle only"],"Aqueductal stenosis leads to non-communicating hydrocephalus, with an enlarged third and lateral ventricles and a normal fourth ventricle."],["q-fcps-ana5-67","A patient cannot look upwards and has convergence-retraction nystagmus and light-near dissociation. The lesion is in the:",["Dorsal midbrain (Parinaud syndrome)","Medial pons","Lateral medulla","Occipital cortex"],"A pineal tumour can compress the superior colliculi and the pretectal area."],["q-fcps-ana5-68","Which nucleus in the hypothalamus is the principal circadian pacemaker?",["Suprachiasmatic nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Mamillary body"],"It receives retinal input and drives melatonin release from the pineal gland."],["q-fcps-ana5-69","The mamillary bodies receive fibres from the hippocampus via the:",["Fornix","Stria terminalis","Cingulum","Uncinate fasciculus"],"Damage to the mamillary bodies and thalamus in thiamine deficiency causes Korsakoff amnesia."],["q-fcps-ana5-70","A patient with Frey syndrome sweats over the cheek while eating. The nerve involved is the:",["Auriculotemporal nerve","Facial nerve","Great auricular nerve","Lingual nerve"],"After parotid surgery regenerating parasympathetic fibres in the auriculotemporal nerve innervate sweat glands."],["q-fcps-ana5-71","The facial nerve divides within which gland?",["Parotid gland","Submandibular gland","Sublingual gland","Thyroid gland"],"The nerve passes through the gland and divides into temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular and cervical branches without supplying it."],["q-fcps-ana5-72","The submandibular duct (Wharton's duct) opens:",["On the sublingual papilla beside the frenulum","Opposite the upper second molar","At the foramen caecum","At the circumvallate papillae"],"Stones are common in this gland because of its long, upward-running duct and its viscous, mucous secretion."],["q-fcps-ana5-73","Which muscle elevates the floor of the mouth and the hyoid during swallowing and is supplied by the trigeminal nerve?",["Mylohyoid","Stylopharyngeus","Sternohyoid","Omohyoid"],"Mylohyoid and the anterior belly of digastric are supplied by the nerve to mylohyoid from V3."],["q-fcps-ana5-74","Zenker's diverticulum arises through the gap between which muscles?",["Thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus (Killian's dehiscence)","Superior and middle constrictors","Stylopharyngeus and palatopharyngeus","Cricothyroid and thyroarytenoid"],"A pulsion diverticulum forms through the weak area above the cricopharyngeus and causes halitosis and regurgitation of undigested food."],["q-fcps-ana5-75","The lymph from the tip of the tongue drains to the:",["Submental nodes","Submandibular nodes","Deep cervical nodes directly","Parotid nodes"],"The tip drains to the submental nodes, the sides to the submandibular nodes and the posterior third to the jugulodigastric nodes."],["q-fcps-ana5-76","The temporomandibular joint dislocates when the:",["Head of the mandible slides in front of the articular tubercle","Condyle moves backward","Disc ruptures","Coronoid process fractures"],"Wide opening, as in yawning, may cause dislocation, and the lateral pterygoid muscle protrudes the mandible."],["q-fcps-ana5-77","Which muscle is the only muscle of mastication that opens the mouth?",["Lateral pterygoid","Medial pterygoid","Masseter","Temporalis"],"The lateral pterygoid depresses and protrudes the mandible. The other muscles close the jaw."],["q-fcps-ana5-78","The chorda tympani carries:",["Taste from the anterior two thirds of the tongue and secretomotor fibres to the submandibular gland","General sensation from the posterior tongue","Motor fibres to the stapedius","Secretomotor fibres to the parotid"],"It branches from the facial nerve in the temporal bone, crosses the tympanic membrane and joins the lingual nerve."],["q-fcps-ana5-79","Which muscle in the middle ear is supplied by the mandibular nerve?",["Tensor tympani","Stapedius","Tensor veli palatini only","Levator veli palatini"],"Tensor tympani (V3) dampens loud sounds, and stapedius (VII) does likewise."],["q-fcps-ana5-80","Otitis media in children spreads to the mastoid air cells through the:",["Aditus to the mastoid antrum","Eustachian tube","Oval window","Round window"],"The mastoid antrum communicates with the epitympanic recess through the aditus."],["q-fcps-ana5-81","The auditory (Eustachian) tube opens into the nasopharynx. It is opened by:",["Tensor veli palatini","Levator veli palatini","Palatoglossus","Stylopharyngeus"],"In children the tube is shorter and more horizontal, which predisposes them to otitis media."],["q-fcps-ana5-82","The lateral rectus muscle is supplied by the:",["Abducens nerve","Trochlear nerve","Oculomotor nerve","Optic nerve"],"The mnemonic is LR6 SO4 and the rest are supplied by the oculomotor nerve."],["q-fcps-ana5-83","Which structure is responsible for the blind spot of the retina?",["Optic disc","Fovea","Macula lutea","Ora serrata"],"The disc has no photoreceptors and lies about 15 degrees medial to the fovea, so the blind spot is temporal in the visual field."],["q-fcps-ana5-84","The aqueous humour is drained mainly through the:",["Trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm","Ciliary body","Vitreous","Choroid"],"Aqueous is secreted by the ciliary body, passes through the pupil and drains at the iridocorneal angle."],["q-fcps-ana5-85","The neural tube defect with the herniation of meninges and spinal cord through a vertebral defect is:",["Myelomeningocele","Spina bifida occulta","Meningocele","Anencephaly"],"Spina bifida occulta is a bony defect without herniation. Folic acid reduces the risk."],["q-fcps-ana5-86","Which germ layer gives rise to the epidermis and the nervous system?",["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest only"],"Endoderm forms the gut lining and glands, and mesoderm forms muscle, bone, the heart and the kidney."],["q-fcps-ana5-87","The primitive heart tube's bulbus cordis gives rise to the:",["Smooth part of the right ventricle (conus arteriosus)","Left atrium","Left ventricle","Sinus venarum"],"The primitive ventricle forms most of the left ventricle, and the bulbus cordis forms the right ventricle's outflow tract."],["q-fcps-ana5-88","Tetralogy of Fallot results from anterior displacement of the:",["Infundibular (conotruncal) septum","Atrial septum","Ductus arteriosus","Aortopulmonary septum only"],"It produces pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, an overriding aorta and a VSD."],["q-fcps-ana5-89","Failure of fusion of the lateral palatine processes with the nasal septum results in:",["Cleft palate","Cleft lip","Choanal atresia","Macroglossia"],"Cleft lip results from failure of the maxillary prominence to fuse with the medial nasal prominence."],["q-fcps-ana5-90","The urachus becomes which structure after birth?",["Median umbilical ligament","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum venosum"],"A patent urachus causes urine leakage at the umbilicus."],["q-fcps-ana5-91","The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts give rise to the:",["Fallopian tubes, uterus and upper vagina","Epididymis and vas deferens","Seminal vesicles","Ovary"],"In males anti-Müllerian hormone from Sertoli cells causes regression, and the mesonephric duct forms the male internal ducts."],["q-fcps-ana5-92","The pancreatic ventral bud forms the:",["Uncinate process and part of the head","Body and tail","Dorsal duct only","Accessory duct only"],"Failure of the ventral bud to rotate fully around the duodenum causes annular pancreas."],["q-fcps-ana5-93","Which structure develops from the fourth pharyngeal arch cartilages?",["Thyroid cartilage","Hyoid body","Stapes","Meckel's cartilage"],"The fourth and sixth arches form the laryngeal cartilages."],["q-fcps-ana5-94","Which cells of the neural crest give rise to the adrenal medulla?",["Chromaffin cells","Schwann cells","Melanocytes","Odontoblasts"],"Neural crest derivatives also include the dorsal root ganglia, parafollicular C cells and the aorticopulmonary septum."],["q-fcps-ana6-01","Which nerve is injured in fracture of the surgical neck of the humerus?",["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck and supplies deltoid and teres minor; loss gives flattening of the shoulder and numbness over the regimental badge area."],["q-fcps-ana6-02","Fracture of the shaft of the humerus most commonly injures the:",["Radial nerve in the spiral groove","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve"],"Wrist drop results from paralysis of the extensors."],["q-fcps-ana6-03","Injury to the ulnar nerve at the elbow (medial epicondyle) causes:",["Claw hand with loss of sensation over the medial one and a half fingers","Wrist drop","Ape hand","Loss of shoulder abduction"],"Medial epicondyle fracture damages the ulnar nerve in the groove behind it."],["q-fcps-ana6-04","Which muscle is supplied by the median nerve in the hand and forms the thenar eminence?",["Abductor pollicis brevis","Adductor pollicis","Palmar interossei","Dorsal interossei"],"Adductor pollicis is supplied by the ulnar nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-05","The carpal tunnel contains all of the following EXCEPT:",["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Flexor pollicis longus tendon","Flexor digitorum superficialis tendons"],"The ulnar nerve and artery pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum in Guyon canal."],["q-fcps-ana6-06","Which muscle initiates abduction of the arm?",["Supraspinatus","Deltoid","Infraspinatus","Teres major"],"Deltoid takes over from about 15 degrees to 90 degrees; supraspinatus is supplied by the suprascapular nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-07","Which rotator cuff muscle is most commonly torn?",["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Subscapularis"],"Its tendon passes under the acromion and is compressed in impingement."],["q-fcps-ana6-08","Winging of the scapula is due to paralysis of:",["Serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve)","Trapezius only","Rhomboids","Latissimus dorsi"],"The long thoracic nerve runs on the superficial surface of serratus anterior and is injured in mastectomy."],["q-fcps-ana6-09","Erb palsy involves which roots?",["C5 and C6","C7 and C8","C8 and T1","C4 and C5"],"The arm hangs medially rotated and extended in the 'waiter's tip' position."],["q-fcps-ana6-10","Klumpke palsy involves which roots?",["C8 and T1","C5 and C6","C6 and C7","C4 and C5"],"It may be associated with Horner syndrome because of sympathetic fibres from T1."],["q-fcps-ana6-11","The femoral nerve supplies which muscle group?",["Quadriceps femoris","Hamstrings","Adductors","Gluteus maximus"],"The femoral nerve arises from L2 to L4 and gives the saphenous nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-12","Injury to the common peroneal nerve at the neck of the fibula causes:",["Foot drop","Loss of plantar flexion","Loss of knee extension","Loss of hip flexion"],"Dorsiflexion and eversion are lost and sensation over the dorsum of the foot is reduced."],["q-fcps-ana6-13","Which nerve supplies the gluteus maximus?",["Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"],"The superior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus medius and minimus, and its injury gives a Trendelenburg gait."],["q-fcps-ana6-14","The Trendelenburg sign is positive when the pelvis:",["Drops on the unsupported side when standing on the affected leg","Rises on the unsupported side","Tilts forward","Rotates internally"],"Gluteus medius on the stance side fails to stabilise the pelvis."],["q-fcps-ana6-15","The blood supply of the head of the femur in adults is mainly from:",["Medial circumflex femoral artery","Lateral circumflex femoral artery","Obturator artery","Superior gluteal artery"],"Femoral neck fractures risk avascular necrosis because the retinacular vessels are torn."],["q-fcps-ana6-16","The unhappy triad of the knee consists of injury to:",["Anterior cruciate ligament, medial collateral ligament and medial meniscus","Posterior cruciate, lateral collateral and lateral meniscus","ACL, PCL and patellar tendon","MCL, LCL and popliteus"],"A valgus blow to the lateral knee produces it."],["q-fcps-ana6-17","The anterior cruciate ligament prevents:",["Anterior displacement of the tibia on the femur","Posterior displacement of the tibia","Hyperextension of the hip","Lateral rotation of the femur"],"A positive anterior drawer test indicates a tear."],["q-fcps-ana6-18","Which structure passes through the foramen ovale?",["Mandibular nerve","Maxillary nerve","Ophthalmic nerve","Middle meningeal artery"],"The middle meningeal artery passes through the foramen spinosum."],["q-fcps-ana6-19","Which cranial nerve passes through the foramen rotundum?",["Maxillary division of the trigeminal","Mandibular division","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"],"The ophthalmic nerve passes through the superior orbital fissure."],["q-fcps-ana6-20","Structures passing through the jugular foramen include:",["Glossopharyngeal, vagus and accessory nerves","Facial and vestibulocochlear nerves","Hypoglossal nerve","Abducens nerve"],"The hypoglossal nerve leaves through the hypoglossal canal."],["q-fcps-ana6-21","The facial nerve leaves the skull through the:",["Stylomastoid foramen","Foramen spinosum","Jugular foramen","Foramen lacerum"],"It first runs in the facial canal of the temporal bone."],["q-fcps-ana6-22","Which muscle of the eye is supplied by the abducens nerve?",["Lateral rectus","Superior oblique","Medial rectus","Inferior oblique"],"LR6 SO4 and the rest by the oculomotor nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-23","A 'down and out' eye with ptosis and a dilated pupil indicates palsy of the:",["Oculomotor nerve","Trochlear nerve","Abducens nerve","Optic nerve"],"A posterior communicating artery aneurysm commonly compresses it."],["q-fcps-ana6-24","Trochlear nerve is unique among cranial nerves because it:",["Emerges from the dorsal surface of the brainstem and decussates","Supplies the lateral rectus","Is purely sensory","Is the longest cranial nerve"],"It supplies the contralateral superior oblique."],["q-fcps-ana6-25","The sensory supply of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue for general sensation is by:",["Lingual nerve (V3)","Chorda tympani","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"Chorda tympani carries taste from the same area."],["q-fcps-ana6-26","All muscles of the tongue are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve EXCEPT:",["Palatoglossus","Genioglossus","Hyoglossus","Styloglossus"],"Palatoglossus is supplied by the pharyngeal plexus (vagus)."],["q-fcps-ana6-27","Paralysis of genioglossus on one side causes the tongue to deviate:",["Towards the side of the lesion","Away from the side of the lesion","Upward","Downward"],"The unopposed healthy genioglossus pushes the tongue towards the weak side."],["q-fcps-ana6-28","The parotid duct opens opposite the:",["Upper second molar tooth","Lower first molar","Upper canine","Lower third molar"],"It pierces buccinator."],["q-fcps-ana6-29","Which gland is the largest salivary gland and is supplied secretomotor fibres from the glossopharyngeal nerve?",["Parotid gland","Submandibular gland","Sublingual gland","Von Ebner glands"],"Preganglionic fibres relay in the otic ganglion via the lesser petrosal nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-30","The only muscle that abducts the vocal cords is:",["Posterior cricoarytenoid","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Cricothyroid","Thyroarytenoid"],"All intrinsic laryngeal muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve except cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-31","Injury to the external laryngeal nerve during thyroidectomy causes:",["Voice weakness and loss of high pitch","Aphonia with stridor","Complete airway obstruction","Loss of cough only"],"Cricothyroid tenses the vocal cords."],["q-fcps-ana6-32","The inferior thyroid artery is closely related to the:",["Recurrent laryngeal nerve","External laryngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Phrenic nerve"],"The superior thyroid artery is related to the external laryngeal nerve."],["q-fcps-ana6-33","The thoracic duct drains into the:",["Junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins","Right brachiocephalic vein","Superior vena cava","Azygos vein"],"It begins at the cisterna chyli at L1 and drains lymph from everything below the diaphragm and the left upper body."],["q-fcps-ana6-34","The azygos vein drains into the:",["Superior vena cava","Inferior vena cava","Right atrium","Left brachiocephalic vein"],"It arches over the root of the right lung at T4."],["q-fcps-ana6-35","The phrenic nerve arises from:",["C3, C4 and C5","C1 and C2","C5 to C7","T1 and T2"],"'C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive.'"],["q-fcps-ana6-36","The aortic opening of the diaphragm lies at the level of:",["T12","T8","T10","L2"],"The vena caval opening is at T8 and the oesophageal opening at T10."],["q-fcps-ana6-37","The coronary sinus drains into the:",["Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Superior vena cava"],"The great cardiac vein runs along the anterior interventricular groove and then becomes the coronary sinus."],["q-fcps-ana6-38","The sinoatrial node is supplied by the right coronary artery in about:",["60% of people","10%","30%","100%"],"In the rest it is supplied by the left circumflex artery."],["q-fcps-ana6-39","The posterior interventricular artery arises from the right coronary artery in:",["Right-dominant circulation (about 70%)","Left-dominant circulation","Co-dominant only","All people"],"Dominance is defined by which artery gives the posterior descending artery."],["q-fcps-ana6-40","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the:",["Arch of the aorta (ligamentum arteriosum)","Right subclavian artery","Brachiocephalic trunk","Pulmonary trunk"],"The right nerve hooks around the right subclavian artery."],["q-fcps-ana6-41","The oesophagus is constricted at all of these sites EXCEPT:",["Gastro-oesophageal junction at T12","Cricoid cartilage (C6)","Crossing of the aortic arch","Crossing of the left main bronchus"],"The diaphragmatic constriction is at the oesophageal hiatus at T10."],["q-fcps-ana6-42","Which part of the stomach is most dependent in the supine position and where aspirated material collects?",["Fundus","Pylorus","Cardia","Antrum"],"In the supine position the fundus is posterior."],["q-fcps-ana6-43","The blood supply of the foregut ends at the:",["Second part of the duodenum (major papilla)","Pylorus","Ileocaecal valve","Splenic flexure"],"The coeliac trunk supplies derivatives of the foregut; the SMA supplies the midgut."],["q-fcps-ana6-44","The superior mesenteric artery arises at the level of:",["L1","T12","L3","L4"],"The coeliac trunk arises at T12 and the inferior mesenteric artery at L3."],["q-fcps-ana6-45","Which structure lies between the SMA and the aorta and may be compressed in SMA syndrome?",["Third part of the duodenum","Second part of the duodenum","Splenic vein","Right renal vein"],"Weight loss reduces the mesenteric fat pad and narrows the angle."],["q-fcps-ana6-46","The portal vein is formed by the union of the:",["Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein","Inferior mesenteric and splenic veins","Left and right gastric veins","Superior and inferior mesenteric veins"],"It forms behind the neck of the pancreas."],["q-fcps-ana6-47","Which is a site of portosystemic anastomosis?",["Lower oesophagus","Upper oesophagus","Duodenum","Appendix"],"Others are the anal canal, the umbilicus (caput medusae) and the retroperitoneum."],["q-fcps-ana6-48","The lymph from the testis drains to the:",["Para-aortic nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Internal iliac nodes","External iliac nodes"],"The scrotal skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes."],["q-fcps-ana6-49","The ureter is crossed by the uterine artery in the:",["Base of the broad ligament ('water under the bridge')","Infundibulopelvic ligament","Round ligament","Uterosacral ligament"],"It lies about 2 cm lateral to the cervix and can be injured in hysterectomy."],["q-fcps-ana6-50","The ovarian artery arises from the:",["Abdominal aorta","Internal iliac artery","Renal artery","Uterine artery"],"The left ovarian vein drains into the left renal vein and the right ovarian vein into the IVC."],["q-fcps-ana6-51","Which ligament carries the ovarian vessels?",["Suspensory ligament of the ovary","Round ligament of the uterus","Cardinal ligament","Ligament of the ovary"],"The round ligament runs to the labium majus through the inguinal canal."],["q-fcps-ana6-52","The pudendal nerve passes through the:",["Greater and lesser sciatic foramina","Obturator canal","Femoral canal","Inguinal canal"],"It runs in the pudendal canal (Alcock canal) and is blocked at the ischial spine."],["q-fcps-ana6-53","Pain from the uterine body travels with sympathetic fibres to which spinal segments?",["T10 to L1","S2 to S4","T5 to T9","L2 to L4"],"Cervical pain follows parasympathetic fibres (S2 to S4)."],["q-fcps-ana6-54","The tentorium cerebelli separates the:",["Cerebrum from the cerebellum","The two cerebral hemispheres","The two cerebellar hemispheres","Pituitary from the brain"],"The falx cerebri separates the cerebral hemispheres."],["q-fcps-ana6-55","The cavernous sinus contains within its lumen:",["Internal carotid artery and abducens nerve","Oculomotor nerve","Trochlear nerve","Maxillary nerve"],"The other nerves lie in its lateral wall."],["q-fcps-ana6-56","Which artery is the commonest cause of extradural haematoma?",["Middle meningeal artery","Bridging veins","Anterior cerebral artery","Basilar artery"],"It is associated with a fracture of the pterion and shows a lens-shaped lesion on CT."],["q-fcps-ana6-57","The circle of Willis is formed in the:",["Interpeduncular cistern (subarachnoid space)","Lateral ventricle","Extradural space","Subdural space"],"The anterior communicating artery connects the two anterior cerebral arteries."],["q-fcps-ana6-58","The most common site of berry aneurysm is the:",["Junction of the anterior communicating and anterior cerebral arteries","Basilar tip","Middle cerebral artery bifurcation","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"],"Rupture causes subarachnoid haemorrhage."],["q-fcps-ana6-59","Occlusion of the middle cerebral artery produces:",["Contralateral hemiparesis and sensory loss, face and arm more than leg","Leg weakness more than arm","Homonymous hemianopia only","Ataxia with vertigo"],"Anterior cerebral artery occlusion affects the leg more."],["q-fcps-ana6-60","Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome is caused by occlusion of the:",["Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","Basilar artery","Anterior spinal artery"],"It gives ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss, with contralateral body loss, and Horner syndrome."],["q-fcps-ana6-61","The area of Broca lies in the:",["Inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere","Superior temporal gyrus","Angular gyrus","Precentral gyrus"],"Damage causes non-fluent expressive aphasia with intact comprehension."],["q-fcps-ana6-62","Wernicke aphasia is due to a lesion in the:",["Posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus","Inferior frontal gyrus","Arcuate fasciculus only","Occipital lobe"],"Speech is fluent but meaningless, with poor comprehension."],["q-fcps-ana6-63","The primary visual cortex is located in:",["Banks of the calcarine sulcus","Parietal lobe","Postcentral gyrus","Superior temporal gyrus"],"A lesion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing in posterior cerebral artery occlusion."],["q-fcps-ana6-64","Bitemporal hemianopia suggests a lesion of the:",["Optic chiasma","Optic tract","Optic radiation","Occipital cortex"],"A pituitary adenoma compresses the decussating nasal-retina fibres."],["q-fcps-ana6-65","The corticospinal tract decussates at the:",["Lower medulla (pyramids)","Pons","Midbrain","Spinal cord"],"Lesions above the decussation cause contralateral weakness."],["q-fcps-ana6-66","The dorsal columns carry:",["Fine touch, vibration and proprioception","Pain and temperature","Crude touch only","Motor fibres"],"They synapse in the nucleus gracilis and cuneatus and decussate in the medulla."],["q-fcps-ana6-67","The spinothalamic tract decussates:",["Within one or two segments of entry, in the anterior white commissure","In the medulla","In the pons","In the thalamus"],"A unilateral cord lesion gives contralateral pain and temperature loss."],["q-fcps-ana6-68","Brown-Sequard syndrome causes:",["Ipsilateral weakness and proprioception loss with contralateral pain and temperature loss","Bilateral weakness","Contralateral weakness","Complete sensory loss only"],"It results from cord hemisection."],["q-fcps-ana6-69","The spinal cord ends in the adult at the level of:",["L1 to L2 (conus medullaris)","S2","L4","T12"],"Lumbar puncture is done at L3-L4 or L4-L5."],["q-fcps-ana6-70","Structures pierced by a lumbar puncture needle in the midline include all EXCEPT:",["Posterior longitudinal ligament","Supraspinous ligament","Ligamentum flavum","Dura mater"],"The posterior longitudinal ligament lies on the anterior wall of the vertebral canal."],["q-fcps-ana6-71","The line joining the highest points of the iliac crests passes through:",["L4 spinous process","L2","S1","L5 vertebral body"],"It is the Tuffier line."],["q-fcps-ana6-72","Which muscle forms the floor of the posterior triangle of the neck partly and is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve?",["Trapezius","Splenius capitis","Levator scapulae","Scalenus medius"],"The accessory nerve is at risk in lymph node biopsy in the posterior triangle."],["q-fcps-ana6-73","Which artery is the main source of blood to the brain via the vertebral system and is a branch of the subclavian artery?",["Vertebral artery","Common carotid artery","Thyrocervical trunk","Internal thoracic artery"],"The vertebral arteries enter the transverse foramen of C6 and join to form the basilar artery."],["q-fcps-ana6-74","The median umbilical ligament is a remnant of the:",["Urachus","Umbilical artery","Umbilical vein","Vitelline duct"],"The medial umbilical ligaments are remnants of the umbilical arteries."],["q-fcps-ana6-75","A persistent vitello-intestinal duct produces:",["Meckel diverticulum","Intussusception","Hirschsprung disease","Malrotation"],"It follows the rule of twos: 2 percent, 2 feet from the ileocaecal valve, 2 inches long."],["q-fcps-ana6-76","The ductus arteriosus connects the:",["Pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch","Right and left atria","Umbilical vein and IVC","Aorta and IVC"],"Its closure is triggered by a rise in oxygen tension and a fall in prostaglandins."],["q-fcps-ana6-77","The foramen ovale lies between the:",["Right and left atria","Right and left ventricles","Aorta and pulmonary trunk","Atrium and ventricle"],"Failure of the septum primum and secundum to fuse leaves a patent foramen ovale."],["q-fcps-ana6-78","The first pharyngeal arch cartilage forms:",["Malleus and incus","Stapes","Hyoid bone","Thyroid cartilage"],"The stapes comes from the second arch (Reichert cartilage)."],["q-fcps-ana6-79","The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the:",["Third pharyngeal pouch","Fourth pharyngeal pouch","Second pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal pouch"],"The thymus is also from the third pouch; the superior parathyroid glands come from the fourth pouch."],["q-fcps-ana6-80","DiGeorge syndrome results from failure of development of the:",["Third and fourth pharyngeal pouches","First pouch","Second pouch","First arch"],"It causes thymic aplasia, hypocalcaemia and cardiac outflow defects, with 22q11 deletion."],["q-fcps-ana6-81","The thyroglossal duct cyst arises from the:",["Foramen caecum remnant of the thyroid descent","Third pharyngeal cleft","Second pharyngeal cleft","Second pouch"],"It moves upwards on tongue protrusion and lies in the midline."],["q-fcps-ana6-82","Cleft lip results from failure of fusion of the:",["Maxillary prominence with the medial nasal prominence","Two mandibular prominences","Palatine shelves","Frontonasal and mandibular prominences"],"Cleft palate is due to failure of the palatine shelves to fuse."],["q-fcps-ana6-83","Neural tube defects are reduced by periconceptional:",["Folic acid","Vitamin B12","Vitamin A","Iron"],"The anterior neuropore closes at about day 25 and the posterior at day 27."],["q-fcps-ana6-84","The notochord persists in the adult as the:",["Nucleus pulposus","Annulus fibrosus","Ligamentum flavum","Vertebral body"],"Remnants may give rise to chordoma."],["q-fcps-ana6-85","Down syndrome most commonly results from:",["Meiotic non-disjunction of chromosome 21","Robertsonian translocation only","Deletion of 21q","Mosaicism"],"The risk rises with maternal age."],["q-fcps-ana6-86","Turner syndrome is:",["45,X","47,XXY","47,XYY","46,XY"],"Features are short stature, webbed neck, streak ovaries and coarctation of the aorta."],["q-fcps-ana6-87","The proximal part of the male urethra above the ejaculatory ducts is the:",["Prostatic urethra","Membranous urethra","Spongy urethra","Navicular fossa"],"The membranous urethra is the narrowest and the least distensible part."],["q-fcps-ana6-88","In straddle injury or pelvic fracture, rupture of the bulbar urethra leads to extravasation of urine into the:",["Superficial perineal pouch","Deep perineal pouch","Ischioanal fossa","Retropubic space"],"Colles fascia limits the spread to the perineum, scrotum, penis and lower abdominal wall."],["q-fcps-ana6-89","The Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle is bounded laterally by the:",["Inferior epigastric vessels","Femoral artery","Deep inguinal ring","Pubic crest"],"A direct hernia passes through it, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels."],["q-fcps-ana6-90","The femoral ring is bounded laterally by the:",["Femoral vein","Lacunar ligament","Inguinal ligament","Pectineal ligament"],"Femoral hernias are more common in women and carry a high risk of strangulation."],["q-fcps-ana6-91","The nerve supply of the skin over the lateral thigh is by the:",["Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve"],"Entrapment causes meralgia paraesthetica."],["q-fcps-ana6-92","The tibial nerve passes behind the medial malleolus through the:",["Tarsal tunnel","Carpal tunnel","Cubital tunnel","Adductor canal"],"Tarsal tunnel syndrome causes burning pain in the sole."],["q-fcps-ana6-93","The dorsalis pedis artery is a continuation of the:",["Anterior tibial artery","Posterior tibial artery","Peroneal artery","Popliteal artery"],"Its pulse lies lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon."],["q-fcps-ana6-94","The great saphenous vein ascends:",["Anterior to the medial malleolus","Behind the lateral malleolus","Behind the medial malleolus","In front of the lateral malleolus"],"It is used for cut-down at the ankle and for bypass grafts, and the small saphenous vein ascends behind the lateral malleolus."],["q-fcps-ana6-95","The small saphenous vein drains into the:",["Popliteal vein","Femoral vein","Great saphenous vein at the saphenofemoral junction","Profunda femoris vein"],"The sural nerve is closely related and may be damaged in its surgery."]];
